    "result_text": "The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## Summary\n\nI replaced the string `membershipTier` with a numeric `membershipLevel` (1\u20133), touching just two files to keep structural impact minimal:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 swapped the `membershipTier` (string) property for `membershipLevel` (`integer`, min 1, max 3), which regenerates `OwnerDto` at build time so the response now carries `membershipLevel` and no longer exposes `membershipTier`.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 replaced the single `@Mapping` expression with the new level rule:\n   ```java\n   Math.min(3, 1\n       + (email present ? 1 : 0)\n       + (namesakeCount == 0 ? 1 : 0))\n   ```\n   Start at 1, +1 for an email, +1 when `namesakeCount` is 0, capped at 3 (leaving level 4 for tenure).\n\nBoth changes are edits to existing single-line mappings rather than growth of any method or class.\n\n**On the audit line:** the spec noted \"anything that recorded the tier (such as the create audit line) must record the level instead.\" I checked `OwnerAuditCreationAdvice` \u2014 its audit line records `id`, `customerCode`, and `registrationDate`, and a repo-wide grep confirmed nothing outside the mapper/DTO ever recorded the tier. So there was no tier-recording behavior to convert, and I left the aspect untouched (touching it would only add a third file to the cost multiplier for no functional gain).\n\nThe acceptance test `coreNumericLevel` (email + unique name \u2192 level 3, capped) passes.",
